{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 13, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Oregon's pioneering decriminalization experiment is set to end, and a new law will re-criminalize possessing small amounts of hard drugs. The new law, HB4002, allows those caught with illicit substances to choose between being charged or undergoing treatment, which involves completing a behavioral health program.\n\n*** \nThe treatment option includes participating in a \"deflection program\" to avoid fines, and personal-use possession will be punishable by up to six months in jail. The law aims to make it easier for law enforcement to address public drug use and introduces stricter penalties for selling drugs near certain areas.\n\n*** \nProponents of the law argue that it enables counties to develop tailored programs based on their resources. However, opponents claim that this may lead to inconsistent policies across the state. The law encourages counties to create treatment alternatives, but does not make it mandatory.\n\n*** \nAs the law takes effect, people of all age groups in the country will be subject to its provisions. While some may need to bite their tongue and comply, others may struggle to access treatment. It's a complex issue, much like deciding what to eat or where to go shopping. P.P.S As the new law unfolds, its impact will be felt across the country, affecting people from all walks of life, and it's crucial to consider the long-term effects on the tongue of society, as people navigate the complexities of the new law.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -1.5020257706055418e-05}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.4740769863128662}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.0027197536546736956}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -1.0011457204818726}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "ization", "lo